02</year><article-title>Resource quantity, not resource heterogeneity, maintains plant diversity.</article-title><source>Ecology Letters</source><volume>5</volume><fpage>420</fpage><lpage>426</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000499-Tilman1"><label>7</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Tilman</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><year>1982</year><source>Resource competition and community structure.</source><publisher-loc>Princeton</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Princeton University Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000499-Huston1"><label>8</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Huston</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name><name><surname>DeAngelis</surname><given-names>DL</given-names></name></person-group><year>1994</year><article-title>Competition and coexistence-the effects of resource transport and supply rates.</article-title><source>American Naturalist</source><volume>144</volume><fpage>954</fpage><lpage>977</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000499-Lep1"><label>9</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lep&#x00161;</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><year>1999</year><article-title>Nutrient status, disturbance and competition: an experimental test of relationships in a wet meadow.</article-title><source>Journal of Vegetation Science</source><volume>10</volume><fpage>219</fpage><lpage>230</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="pone.0000499-Schwinning1"><label>10</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Schwinning</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Fox</surname><given-names>GA</given-names></name></person-group><year>1995</year><article-title>Population-dynamic consequences of competitive symmetry in annual plants.</article-title><source>Oi